# A [[strip#Noun|strip]] of [[fabric]], especially from the [[edge#Noun|edge]] of a [[piece#Noun|piece]] of [[cloth]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Measure|act=I|scene=ii|page=62|column=1|lines=27–34|passage=1. ''Gent''[''leman'']. Well: there went but a paire of [[shears|ſheeres]] betweene vs. / ''Luc''[''io'']. I grant: as there may betweene the '''Liſts''', and the [[velvet|Veluet]]. Thou art the '''Liſt'''. / 1. ''Gent''. And thou the Veluet. Thou art good Veluet; thou'rt a three [[piled|pild]]-piece I warrant thee: I had as [[lief]]e be a '''Lyſt''' of an Engliſh [[kersey|Kerſey]], as be pil'd, as thou art pil'd, for a French Veluet. Do I ſpeake feelingly now?|translation=''1st Gentleman''. Well, you and I are [[cut from the same cloth]]. / ''Lucio''. I agree: just as the '''lists''' [scraps from the edge of the cloth] and the velvet are from the same cloth. You are the '''list'''. / ''1st Gentleman''. And you are the velvet. You are good velvet; you are a three-piled piece, I'll bet. I would willingly be a '''list''' of an English kersey, than be full of [[piles]] {{q-q|[[haemorrhoid]]s}}, as you are piled, like a French velvet. Do I speak feelingly now?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1889|year_published=1899|author={{w|Rudyard Kipling}}|title={{w|Under the Deodars}}|chapter=The Education of Otis Yeere|location= Boston|publisher=The Greenock Press|page=20|text=“Listen! I see it all — down, down even to the stays! ''Such'' stays! Six-eight a pair, Polly, with red flannel — or '''list''' is it? — that they put into the tops of those fearful things. I can draw you a picture of them.”}}
#* {{RQ:Doyle Poison Belt|passage=Why should we not send a message out over London which would attract to us anyone who might still be alive? I ran across, and pulling at the '''list'''-covered rope, I was surprised to find how difficult it was to swing the bell.}}
# {{senseid|en|material cloth}} [[material#Noun|Material]] used for cloth [[selvage]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=The Jewish New Year|magazine=The Jewish Herald: A Record of Christian Effort for the Salvation of Israel|location=London|publisher=John Snow & Co.,{{nb...|2, Ivy Lane, Paternoster Row}}; and the [[w:British Society for the Propagation of the Gospel Among the Jews|British Society [for the Propagation of the Gospel Among the Jews]]],{{nb...|96, Great Russell Street, Bloomsbury.}}|date=18 September 1871|year_published=1 November 1871|page=174|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=KUEEAAAAQAAJ&pg=RA1-PA174|oclc=749304689|passage=Previous to the offering up of prayer, however, the persons chosen for this office [of praying for the people] had divested themselves of their boots and put on '''list''' slippers, their hands being washed by "the descendants of Levi" at a basin near the Holy of Holies.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Arthur Conan Doyle|A[rthur] Conan Doyle]]|chapter=[[w:The Adventure of the Naval Treaty|The Naval Treaty]]|title=w:The Memoirs of Sherlock Holmes|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:A. L. Burt|year=1893|oclc=691143|location2=London|publisher2=[[w:John Murray (publisher)|John Murray]],{{nb...|Albemarle Street, W}}|year2=January 1950|page2=255|pageurl2=https://archive.org/stream/in.ernet.dli.2015.274267/2015.274267.The-Memoirs#page/n263/mode/1up|oclc2=632221174|passage="How is it, then, that the woman who came into the room about nine left to traces with her muddy boots?" / "I am glad you raise the point. It occurred to me at the time. The charwomen are in the habit of taking off their boots at the commissionaire's office, and putting on '''list''' slippers."}}
# {{senseid|en|register}} A [[register#Noun|register]] or [[roll#Noun|roll]] of [[paper#Noun|paper]] consisting of a [[compilation]] or [[enumeration]] of a [[set#Noun|set]] of possible [[item]]s; the compilation or enumeration itself. {{defdate|from 1600}}
#* {{RQ:Bacon Essayes|chapter=Of Youth and Age. XLII.|pages=247–248|pageref=247|passage=Natures that haue much Heat, and great and violent deſires and Perturbations, are not ripe for Action, till they haue paſſed the Meridian of their yeares: As it was with ''[[w:Julius Caesar|Iulius Cæſar]]'', and ''[[w:Septimius Severus|Septimius Seuerus]]''. {{...}} And yet he [Septimus Severus] was the Ableſt Emperour, almoſt, of all the '''Liſt'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Christmas Carol|pages=11–12|pageref=12|passage="Scrooge and Marley's, I believe," said one of the gentlemen, referring to his '''list'''. "Have I the pleasure of addressing Mr. Scrooge, or Mr. Marley?"}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|title=A punch in the gut: How microbes promote liver cancer in the overweight|url=https://www.economist.com/science-and-technology/2013/06/29/a-punch-in-the-gut|date=29 June 2013|volume=407|issue=8842|pages=72–73|passage=Mostly, the microbiome is beneficial. {{...}} Research over the past few years, however, has implicated it in diseases from atherosclerosis to asthma to autism. Dr Yoshimoto and his colleagues would like to add liver cancer to that '''list'''.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230727064628/https://www.economist.com/science-and-technology/2013/06/29/a-punch-in-the-gut|archivedate=27 July 2023}}
# {{senseid|en|barrier}}{{lb|en|in the plural|historical}} The [[barrier]]s or [[palisade]]s used to [[fence off]] a [[space#Noun|space]] for [[jousting#Noun|jousting]] or [[tilting#Noun|tilting]] [[tournament]]s.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 2|I|3|text=On pain of death, no person be so bold<br>Or daring-hardy as to touch the '''lists''',<br>Except the marshal and such officers<br>Appointed to direct these fair designs.}}
#* {{RQ:Butler Hudibras|year=1905|part=1|canto=II|page=32|passage=With Truncheon tip'd with Iron head, / The Warrior to the '''Lists''' [he] led; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Homer Pope Iliad|volume=I|book=III|page=16|lines=319–323|passage=Ariſe, O Father of the ''Trojan'' State! / The Nations call, thy joyful People wait, / To ſeal the Truce and end the dire Debate. / ''Paris'' thy Son, and ''Sparta''’s King advance, / In meaſur’d '''Liſts''' to toſs the weighty Lance; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Ivanhoe|volume=I|chapter=VIII|page=139|passage=William de Wyvil, and Stephen de Martival, {{...}} armed at all points, rode up and down the '''lists''' to enforce and preserve good order among the spectators.}}
#* {{RQ:C. S. Lewis Prince Caspian|chapter=Chapter 14. How All Were Very Busy|text=They ran down to the '''lists''' and Peter came outside the ropes to meet them, his face red and sweaty, his chest heaving.}}
# {{lb|en|in the plural|military|historical}} The [[scene]] of a [[military]] [[contest]]; the [[ground]] or [[field]] of [[combat]]; an [[enclosed]] [[space]] that serves as a [[battlefield]]; the [[site]] of a [[pitched battle]].
#* {{Q|en|John Williamson Palmer|{{w|Stonewall Jackson's Way|Stonewall Jackson's Way}}|quote=The sun’s bright lances rout the mists of morning, and by George! Here’s Longstreet struggling in the '''lists''', hemmed in an ugly gorge. Pope and his Yankees, whipped before, “Bay’nets and grape!” hear Stonewall roar; “Charge, Stuart! Pay off Ashby’s score!” in “Stonewall Jackson’s Way.”|year=1862}}
# {{lb|en|data structure}} A [[codified#Adjective|codified]] [[representation]] of a [[Wikipedia:List (abstract data type)|list]] used to [[store#Verb|store]] [[data]] or in [[processing#Noun|processing]]; especially, in the [[Lisp]] [[programming language]], a [[data structure]] consisting of a [[sequence#Noun|sequence]] of [[zero]] or more items.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Ed Acly|title=A Tale of Three Languages: C, Ada & Lisp|magazine=[[w:Computerworld|Computerworld: The Newsweekly for the Computer Community]]|location=Framingham, Mass.|publisher=CW Communications|date=10 March 1985|volume=XIX|issue=12|page=ID/10|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=EFea26Iy2kQC&pg=PA62|columns=1–2|issn=0010-4841|oclc=234191689|passage=Lisp is an applicative language. This means that it is structured around applying functions (operations) to a linked '''list''' of arguments that accompany those functions. {{...}} A function call or function definition is only coded in the syntax of a '''list''', which can be of an indefinite length. Thus, the '''list''' is the only data structure for a Lisp program.}}
# {{lb|en|architecture}} A little [[square#Adjective|square]] [[moulding#Noun|moulding]]; a [[fillet]] or [[listel]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[John Carter]|chapter=STRIÆ|title=The Builder’s Magazine: Or, A Universal Dictionary for Architects, Carpenters, Masons, Bricklayers, &c.{{nb...|as well as for Every Gentleman who would Wish to be a Competent Judge of the Elegat and Necessary Art of Building. [...]}}|edition=new|location=London|publisher=Printed for E. Newbery,{{nb...|the corner of St. Paul's Church Yard, in Ludgate Street; and sold by all other booksellers in Great Britain and Ireland.}}|year=1788|page=284|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=TOAX7lqmLbQC&pg=PA284|oclc=12051583|passage=STRIÆ, in ancient architecture, the '''liſts''', fillets or rays which ſeparate the ſtriges or flutings of columns.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Edward Shaw; [[w:Thomas Silloway|Thomas W[illiam] Silloway]]; [[w:George M. Harding|George M[ilford] Harding]]|chapter=Introduction|title=Civil Architecture; being a Complete Theoretical and Practical System of Building, Containing the Fundamental Principles of the Art.{{nb...|[...] To which is Added a Treatise on Gothic Architecture, etc., by Thomas W. Silloway and George M. Harding, Architects.}}|edition=11th|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher=Henry Carey Baird & Co.,{{nb...|industrial publishers, booksellers, and importers, 810 Walnut Street.}}|year=1876|page=22|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=hpNZAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA22|column=2|oclc=191228916|passage=A ''volute'' is a kind of spiral scroll, used in the Ionic and Composite capitals, of which it makes the principal characteristic and ornament. {{...}} There are several diversities practised in the volute. In some, the '''list''' or edge, throughout all the circumvolutions, is in the same line or plane. {{...}} [I]n others, the canal or one circumvolution is detached from the '''list''' of another by a vacuity or aperture.}}
# {{lb|en|carpentry}} A [[narrow#Adjective|narrow]] strip of [[wood]], especially [[sapwood]], cut from the edge of a [[board#Noun|board]] or [[plank#Noun|plank]].
# {{lb|en|ropemaking}} A piece of [[woollen#Adjective|woollen]] cloth with which the [[yarn]]s are [[grasp#Verb|grasped]] by a [[worker]].
# {{lb|en|tin-plate manufacture}} The [[first#Adjective|first]] thin [[coating#Noun|coating]] of [[tin]]; a [[wire#Noun|wire]]-like [[rim#Noun|rim]] of tin left on an edge of the [[plate#Noun|plate]] after it is [[coat#Verb|coated]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[stripe#Noun|stripe]].
#* {{RQ:Browne Pseudodoxia Epidemica|edition=2nd|chapter=Of the Same [''i.e.'', the Blacknesse of Negroes]|page=282|passage=Thus the Aſſe having a peculiar mark of a croſſe made by a black '''liſt''' down his back, and another athwart, or at right angles down his ſhoulders; common opinion aſcribes this figure unto a peculiar ſignation; ſince that beaſt had the honour to bear our Saviour on his back.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[boundary]] or [[limit#Noun|limit]]; a [[border#Noun|border]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-1 Q1|act=IV|scene=i|page=62|passage=[W]ere it good / {{...}} to ſet ſo rich a maine / On the nice hazard of one doubtfull houre? / It were not good for therein ſhould we read / The very bottome and the ſoule of hope, / The very '''liſt''', the very vtmost bound / Of all our fortunes.|translation=Is it good / {{...}} to place so high a stake / On the risky hazard of one doubtful hour? / No, it would be no good for we would read into it that we had reached / The end of our hope, / The very '''limit''', the very utmost boundary / Of all our luck.}}

